<section>
    <h2>Setting the table once</h2>
    <p>
        Most tests need something ready before they start: a user, a basket, a record in the database. NUnit gives
        you two places to make it. <code>[SetUp]</code> runs before <em>every</em> test, so each one gets its own.
        <code>[OneTimeSetUp]</code> runs <em>once</em> for the whole class, for things the tests can share.
    </p>
<pre><code>public class ReportTests : DungeonTest
{
    private static string reportId = "";

    [OneTimeSetUp]                       // once, before any test in this class
    public static async Task CreateReport()
    {
        using var pw = await Microsoft.Playwright.Playwright.CreateAsync();
        var api = await pw.APIRequest.NewContextAsync(new() { BaseURL = DungeonUrl });
        var reply = await api.PostAsync("/api/reports");
        reportId = (await reply.JsonAsync())?.GetProperty("id").GetString() ?? "";
    }

    [SetUp]                              // before every test
    public async Task OpenIt() =&gt; await EnterAsync($"/reports/{reportId}");
}</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Setup through the API, not the UI.</strong> A test that clicks through three screens to make its
        data is slow and breaks when those screens change. An API call makes the same thing in milliseconds.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Clean hands</h2>
    <p>
        Every Playwright test already gets a fresh browser context: its own cookies, its own storage, nothing left
        over from the test before. Keep it that way. A test that depends on what another test left behind passes on
        its own and fails in a crowd.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <p>
        NUnit runs tests one at a time unless you tell it otherwise. Three attributes switch it to parallel:
    </p>
<pre><code>[assembly: LevelOfParallelism(5)]      // up to five tests at once (default: one per CPU core)

[Parallelizable(ParallelScope.All)]   // this class, and every test in it, may run alongside others
[FixtureLifeCycle(LifeCycle.InstancePerTestCase)]   // a new object per test, so each gets its own Page
public class ParallelTests : DungeonTest
{
    [TestCase(1)]
    [TestCase(2)]
    [TestCase(3)]
    public async Task Open(int n) =&gt; await EnterAsync($"/rooms/{n}");
}</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Why one instance per test case?</strong> By default NUnit makes one object per class, and all its
        tests share it. Run them at the same time and they share its <code>Page</code> too: one test clicks on
        another's page. With an instance per test case, shared things have to be <code>static</code>, which is
        exactly what <code>[OneTimeSetUp]</code> fills in.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The Severing Pit</h2>
    <p>
        The hydra has five heads, and they only rear when a crawler stands at every one. Then all five have to fall
        within three seconds, or they grow back. Start one hunt, then send five crawlers, each with clean hands, all
        at once. One after another never works: the hydra won't wait ten seconds for the rest, and a crawler already
        at the pit can't face a second head.
    </p>
</section>
